    },
    {
      "id": "ev_35",
      "evidence_type": "report_text",
      "report_id": "ThreatConnect_KASPERAGENT-Campaign-resurfaces-May-Election(06-14-2017)",
      "locator": {
        "page": 6,
        "section": "body",
        "sentence_id": "p6_s2"
      },
      "text": "Reverse DNS and Passive DNS results for 195.154.110[.]237",
      "language": "en",
      "confidence": 0.95,
      "note": "narrative sentence evidence",
      "source": "document"
    },
    {
      "id": "ev_36",
      "evidence_type": "report_text",
      "report_id": "ThreatConnect_KASPERAGENT-Campaign-resurfaces-May-Election(06-14-2017)",
      "locator": {
        "page": 7,
        "section": "body",
        "sentence_id": "p7_s1"
      },
      "text": "Two of the four domains that have been hosted at this IP since 2016 -- upfile2box[.]com and 7aga[.]net -- were registered by a freelance web developer in Gaza, Palestine.",
      "language": "en",
      "confidence": 0.95,
      "note": "narrative sentence evidence",
      "source": "document"
    },
    {
      "id": "ev_50",
      "evidence_type": "report_text",
      "report_id": "ThreatConnect_KASPERAGENT-Campaign-resurfaces-May-Election(06-14-2017)",
      "locator": {
        "page": 7,
        "section": "body",
        "sentence_id": "p7_s15"
      },
      "text": "More associated indicators, screenshots of many of the decoy documents, and descriptions of the activity are available via the March - May 2017 Kasperagent Malware Leveraging WindowsNewUpdates[.]com Campaign in ThreatConnect.",
      "language": "en",
      "confidence": 0.95,
      "note": "narrative sentence evidence",
      "source": "document"
    },
    {
      "id": "ev_53",
      "evidence_type": "report_text",
      "report_id": "ThreatConnect_KASPERAGENT-Campaign-resurfaces-May-Election(06-14-2017)",
      "locator": {
        "page": 1,
        "section": "rule",
        "sentence_id": "p1_r3"
      },
      "text": "The threat actors used shortened URLs in spear phishing messages and fake news websites to direct targets to download KASPERAGENT. Upon execution, KASPERAGENT drops the payload and a decoy document that displays Arabic names and ID numbers. The malware establishes persistence and sends HTTP requests to the command and control domain mailsinfo[.]net. Of note, the callbacks were to PHP scripts that included /dad5/ in the URLs. Most samples of the malware reportedly function as a basic reconnaissance tool and downloader. However, some of the recently identified files display “extended-capability” including the functionality to steal passwords, take screenshots, log keystrokes, and steal files. These “extended-capability” samples called out to an additional command and control domain, stikerscloud[.]com. Additionally, early variants of KASPERAGENT used “Chrome” as the user agent, while more recent samples use “OPAERA” - a possible misspelling of the “Opera” - browser. The indicators associated with the blog article are available in the ThreatConnect Technical Blogs and Reports source here .",
      "language": "en",
      "confidence": 0.98,
      "note": "rule or indicator snippet evidence",
      "source": "document"
    },
    {
      "id": "ev_54",
      "evidence_type": "report_text",
      "report_id": "ThreatConnect_KASPERAGENT-Campaign-resurfaces-May-Election(06-14-2017)",
      "locator": {
        "page": 2,
        "section": "rule",
        "sentence_id": "p2_r1"
      },
      "text": "first malicious sample we identified ( 6843AE9EAC03F69DF301D024BFDEFC88 ) had the file name “testproj.exe” and was identified within an archive file ( 4FE7561F63A71CA73C26CB95B28EAEE8 ) with the name “اﻟﻜﺎﻣﻠﺔ اﻟﺘﻔﺎﺻﯿﻞ",
      "language": "en",
      "confidence": 0.98,
      "note": "rule or indicator snippet evidence",
      "source": "document"
    },
    {
      "id": "ev_55",
      "evidence_type": "report_text",
      "report_id": "ThreatConnect_KASPERAGENT-Campaign-resurfaces-May-Election(06-14-2017)",
      "locator": {
        "page": 2,
        "section": "rule",
        "sentence_id": "p2_r2"
      },
      "text": "We detonated the file in VxStream’s automated malware analysis capability and found testproj.exe dropped a benign Microsoft Word document that pulls a jpg file from treestower[.]com . Malwr.com observed this site in association with another sample that called out to mailsinfo[.]net - a host identified in the Targeted Attacks in the Middle East Using KASPERAGENT and MICROPSIA blog. That was our first hint that we were looking at KASPERAGENT.",
      "language": "en",
      "confidence": 0.98,
      "note": "rule or indicator snippet evidence",
      "source": "document"
    },
    {
      "id": "ev_56",
      "evidence_type": "report_text",
      "report_id": "ThreatConnect_KASPERAGENT-Campaign-resurfaces-May-Election(06-14-2017)",
      "locator": {
        "page": 2,
        "section": "rule",
        "sentence_id": "p2_r3"
      },
      "text": "The jpg pulled from treestower[.]com displays a graphic picture of a dead man, which also appeared on a Palestinian news website discussing the death of Hamas military leader Mazen Fuqaha. A separate malicious executable - 2DE25306A58D8A5B6CBE8D5E2FC5F3C5 (vlc.exe) - runs when the photograph is displayed, using the YouTube icon and calling out to several URLs on windowsnewupdates[.]com . This host was registered in late March and appears to be unique to this campaign.",
      "language": "en",
      "confidence": 0.98,
      "note": "rule or indicator snippet evidence",
      "source": "document"
    },
    {
      "id": "ev_57",
      "evidence_type": "report_text",
      "report_id": "ThreatConnect_KASPERAGENT-Campaign-resurfaces-May-Election(06-14-2017)",
      "locator": {
        "page": 2,
        "section": "rule",
        "sentence_id": "p2_r4"
      },
      "text": "With our interest piqued, we pivoted on the import hashes (also known as an imphash), which captures the import table of a given file. Shared import hashes across multiple files would likely identify files that are part of the same malware family. We found nine additional samples sharing the imphash values for the two executables, C66F88D2D76D79210D568D7AD7896B45 and DCF3AA484253068D8833C7C5B019B07 .",
      "language": "en",
      "confidence": 0.98,
      "note": "rule or indicator snippet evidence",
      "source": "document"
    },
    {
      "id": "ev_58",
      "evidence_type": "report_text",
      "report_id": "ThreatConnect_KASPERAGENT-Campaign-resurfaces-May-Election(06-14-2017)",
      "locator": {
        "page": 2,
        "section": "rule",
        "sentence_id": "p2_r5"
      },
      "text": "Import Hash Results c66f88d2d76d79210d568d7ad7896b45",
      "language": "en",
      "confidence": 0.98,
      "note": "rule or indicator snippet evidence",
      "source": "document"
    },
    {
      "id": "ev_59",
      "evidence_type": "report_text",
      "report_id": "ThreatConnect_KASPERAGENT-Campaign-resurfaces-May-Election(06-14-2017)",
      "locator": {
        "page": 3,
        "section": "rule",
        "sentence_id": "p3_r1"
      },
      "text": "Import Hash Results dcf3aa484253068d8833c7c5b019b07a",
      "language": "en",
      "confidence": 0.98,
      "note": "rule or indicator snippet evidence",
      "source": "document"
    },
    {
      "id": "ev_60",
      "evidence_type": "report_text",
      "report_id": "ThreatConnect_KASPERAGENT-Campaign-resurfaces-May-Election(06-14-2017)",
      "locator": {
        "page": 3,
        "section": "rule",
        "sentence_id": "p3_r2"
      },
      "text": "Analysis of those files uncovered two more imphashes, 0B4E44256788783634A2B1DADF4F9784 and E44F0BD2ADFB9CBCABCAD314D27ACCFC , for a total of 20 malicious files. These additional samples behaved similarly to the initial files; testproj.exe dropped benign decoy files and started malicious executables. The malicious executables all called out to the same URLs on windowsnewupdates[.]com.",
      "language": "en",
      "confidence": 0.98,
      "note": "rule or indicator snippet evidence",
      "source": "document"
    },
    {
      "id": "ev_62",
      "evidence_type": "report_text",
      "report_id": "ThreatConnect_KASPERAGENT-Campaign-resurfaces-May-Election(06-14-2017)",
      "locator": {
        "page": 5,
        "section": "rule",
        "sentence_id": "p5_r1"
      },
      "text": "We don’t know for sure who is responsible for this campaign, but digging into the passive DNS results led us to some breadcrumbs. Starting with 195.154.110[.]237 , the IP address which is hosting the command and control domain windowsnewupdates[.]com, we found that the host is on a dedicated server.",
      "language": "en",
      "confidence": 0.98,
      "note": "rule or indicator snippet evidence",
      "source": "document"
    },
    {
      "id": "ev_63",
      "evidence_type": "report_text",
      "report_id": "ThreatConnect_KASPERAGENT-Campaign-resurfaces-May-Election(06-14-2017)",
      "locator": {
        "page": 7,
        "section": "rule",
        "sentence_id": "p7_r1"
      },
      "text": "Two of the four domains that have been hosted at this IP since 2016 -- upfile2box[.]com and 7aga[.]net -- were registered by a freelance web developer in Gaza, Palestine. This IP has been used to host a small number of domains, some of which were registered by the same actor, suggesting the IP is dedicated for a single individual or group’s use. While not conclusive, it is intriguing that the same IP was observed hosting a domain ostensibly registered in Gaza AND the command and control domain associated with a series of targeted attacks leveraging Palestinian Authority-themed decoy documents referencing Gaza.",
      "language": "en",
      "confidence": 0.98,
      "note": "rule or indicator snippet evidence",
      "source": "document"
    },
